{"text":"Hi B — C asked me to help you three sort out the trailer. I'm not here to decide anything or push a plan; I want to understand what's actually at stake for each of you first.

C told me "B's pad is where it lives, so she is stuck in the middle." I'd like to hear it in your words. What does having the trailer on your pad mean for you — practically, and in terms of your relationships with A and C? What would you need in order for this to feel manageable rather than like a problem you're absorbing for other people?

And: anything about this arrangement that worries you even slightly? Minor discomforts count — I'd rather hear them now. I'll check what I've heard back with you before it goes on any list.","to":"B"}

{"text":"Thank you — that's genuinely useful, and it isn't letting anyone down to say it. Space you didn't plan to give up is a real cost, and a landlord's answer is a real constraint, not an excuse.

Let me try to put your concern in neutral words and see if it fits: "B wants to be able to say what's kept on her own pad without fearing she's letting her neighbors down — which means knowing it's something she can stand behind with her landlord, and not having her home quietly absorb a burden she didn't choose."

Is that right? Change any word that isn't yours. Two follow-ups, and "no" is a fine answer to either: would you be willing to ask your landlord, even informally, so we know the real constraint instead of guessing? And is there a version of "the trailer isn't behind my house" that you'd actually want, versus just tolerating — e.g. it lives elsewhere and visits occasionally?","to":"B"}

{"text":"Noted, and my mistake — I'll write "her project" from here on.

Two clarifications on your NCE. "Which means a starting point for access and decisions" — is that the part that's off? Try this version: "C needs to be able to count on the trailer for her project in the next few weeks, and needs access and decisions to work by rules that don't depend on everyone agreeing in the moment." Does that fit? And the "won't wait until A is done" — I read that as: your project has a real deadline, A's need is real too, and you don't want to be told to go last. Is that fair, or is there something more specific about why being second in line doesn't work?

Also, in fairness you should know what I told A: that you need it in the same window and won't wait. And that B says she'd rather the trailer lived elsewhere, with occasional visits possibly fine. Your reaction to that last bit is useful to me — you said you didn't know. Anything you want to say about it before I start drafting options?","to":"C"}

{"text":"Then here's what I'll record for you, in your words: "B wants the trailer moved off her pad, and wants that to be possible without feeling she's letting her neighbors down. Occasional visits to the pad might be fine; she's willing to ask her landlord informally about what's allowed."

Does that fit? If so it goes on the list as settled — and if you ever want it changed, say so and I'll change it, no argument.

For transparency: C says if you want it moved, another storage spot needs discussing. A knows too. Nobody has objected to the idea. Would it help if I asked your landlord question in a way that makes clear it's exploratory — you're not committing to anything — or would you rather handle that yourself when you're ready?","to":"B"}

{"text":"Revised, without "fixed deadline": "C needs to be able to count on the trailer for her home project in the next few weeks, and needs access and decisions to work by rules that don't depend on everyone agreeing in the moment — and being asked to wait her turn behind A doesn't work for her."

Does that fit? I'd like to record it as settled. And B has confirmed her side: she wants it moved off her pad without feeling she's letting you two down, occasional visits possibly fine, and she'll ask her landlord herself when she's ready.

So the list so far: C's access-to-it-soon concern, A's plan-my-trips-without-back-and-forth concern (waiting on A's confirmation of wording), and B's pad-and-landlord concern (confirmed). One thing on my own account, which you three get to say yes or no to: A and C both tell me they need it "in the next few weeks" and neither will go second — but neither has said what's actually ruined if it slips a week. That worries me, because "weeks" is vague enough that you two could end up fighting over a collision that isn't real. My concern, in neutral words: "the group can tell the difference between a genuine clash and two calendars that overlap by accident, so that nobody gives up something they need for a conflict that didn't exist." Does that fit as something worth caring about, or would you phrase it differently?","to":"C"}
